1.21.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- An order line shows the goods it sold: a photo, the article, and the title as a link to the product's own page. Checking what somebody actually ordered no longer means searching the catalogue by hand. Both the photo and the link are looked up when the order is opened, never stored — the order's snapshot must not age with the catalogue, and goods deleted since simply stop being a link
- The order card reads like a card: the buyer under a heading with their phone and email as links, the comment in a block of its own, a line per item with its own sum, and the status right there, so an order can be closed without going back to the list
- Sizes in the admin read as sizes again: 32.4 MB, 3 GB, 152 KB. The browser's own compact notation turned a disk into "1,2 млрд Б" in Russian — arithmetically true and unreadable. KB, MB and GB are technical marks and stay in Latin in every language, as do CPU, RAM and HDD
- A changed price needs nothing submitted to a search engine, and now a test says so: the storefront sends no caching headers for a page whose price can move, the markup carries the same number the shopper sees, and the sitemap dates the change so a crawler returns on its own

1.20.2 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The browser's own "broken image" glyph is gone for good: a picture that fails to load is covered by its own mask, which a loaded picture never shows. Moving the glyph out of the frame did not work — it is drawn inside the picture's box and ignores colour, font size and indentation alike
- A dialog freezes the page behind it. A modal takes the clicks but not the wheel, so the list kept scrolling under the card and the window carried two scrollbars side by side
- A price in the product list no longer breaks across two lines, with the currency stranded under the number
- The warning mark for a supplier-hosted photo is back to the one photo a shopper actually sees in the listing. Lit by any of a product's photos it burned on every row of a catalogue that is hotlinked from end to end, and a warning that fires on everything warns of nothing

1.20.1 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The browser's own "broken image" glyph no longer shows in the corner of a tile whose photo did not load: the shop's mark now stands there alone
- The thumbnails under a product's gallery carry the mark too. A strip of blank white squares told the owner nothing about which of the twelve photos is the missing one

1.20.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A photo that fails to load now shows the shop's own "no photo" mark instead of the product's title sprawled across the tile — in the catalogue, on the product page and in the admin's list alike. The frame around every picture carries the mark, and a picture that loads covers it; the storefront does this without a line of JavaScript, which it still does not have
- Nothing is deleted over a photo any more. A supplier's link that answers 404 today can answer perfectly tomorrow — their server comes back, and the shop shows the picture again by itself, with no row to restore. Yesterday's rule that dropped such rows, and the separate link-checking pass added hours ago, are both gone: the display was the problem, and the display is where it is solved

1.19.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- "Check the photos": a pass that asks the supplier which of the hotlinked pictures are still there and drops the ones that are gone, downloading nothing. "Fill in photos" already did this, but on its way to copying every picture onto your disk — and a shop that has not agreed with its supplier about their materials still cannot afford a dead link, which the catalogue renders as the product title sprawled where the photo belongs. Measured on a live catalogue of 69 905 supplier links, about one in twenty five is dead
- What counts as gone is decided in one place for both passes, so the download and the check can never disagree about which photo is a corpse

1.18.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- An order opens as a card: article, product, unit price, quantity and line sum for every line, the buyer's comment in full, and their phone and email as links to call and write from. Until now the whole order was one joined line of titles, and the article and unit price — both kept in the order's own snapshot — never reached the screen
- The order list stopped being set by the longest title in it. One supplier name of two hundred characters made a three-line row and pushed the total half a screen away from the goods it belonged to; a title now takes two lines at most and the rest is in the card
- A phone number no longer breaks in the middle across two lines, and a buyer's comment is visible in the row instead of only in a tooltip nobody hovers over
- The order total is counted by the shop, once. The browser and the accountant's CSV each parsed the stored snapshot their own way, which is how two numbers for one order appear; the raw snapshot now stays on the server, where it is the legal record it was meant to be
- The CSV for the accountant gained the buyer's comment: delivery wishes and a convenient time to call were the one thing the export dropped

1.17.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The shop keeps a log file and hands it to its owner: Status → Expert mode shows its size, when it last moved, and opens it. Until now everything the background did — a photo that would not download, an order email that never left — went to the machine's journal, which the owner of a hosted shop has no way to read. The path is a config key (log_path), and the file is capped the way our other services cap theirs
- A photo the supplier has deleted is dropped instead of retried forever. An imported photo is a link, that link is the only copy, and a dead one made the catalogue render the product's title sprawling where the picture should be; without the row the storefront falls back to its own "no photo" mark. Measured on a live catalogue of 69 905 supplier links, about one in twenty five
- Only 404 and 410 count as gone. A refusal to hotlink, a rate limit, a bad hour at the supplier and an error page served as a photo all keep the link and are tried again — a wider net would erase live photos during somebody else's outage, and there is nothing to restore them from
- "Fill in photos" now reports three numbers: downloaded, gone, still links
- The warning mark on a product now lights up when any of its photos is still on the supplier's server, not only the first one. A product with one photo of its own and two borrowed looked settled

1.16.0 / August 18, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The Wildberries markup ladder got its "Remove" button back. Ozon has had one since the tab was written, and without it a band typed by mistake could only be undone by reloading the page
- Dialogs are the browser's own now: Escape closes them, focus stays inside while they are open, and a screen reader announces them as dialogs. The admin's dialogs had none of that
- Signing in and the first-run setup are real forms: the browser offers the saved password, Enter submits, and the email field is an email field on a phone keyboard
- Confirming "Fill in photos" is one question instead of a dialog holding a single checkbox
- Uploading a price list no longer walks the file byte by byte in the browser; a 143 MB spreadsheet is handed over by the platform instead
- Disk and database sizes in the admin are formatted by the browser's own locale rules, so they read as they do everywhere else in the system
- The shop's language is resolved in one place instead of three copies that had already begun to disagree
- A token row lost a column nothing read: a one-time link is spent by deleting it, which is what made it one-time all along. Existing databases keep the column and are unaffected

1.15.1 / August 17, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The feed gives the category tree as a tree: an element per segment tied by parentId, instead of one flat element named after the whole path. Yandex was reading a shop one level deep, and a catalogue copied between two instances of the shop landed beside the tree rather than inside it, as five new sections with the separator rewritten as a dash
- The catalogue fits a photo into its tile instead of cropping it. A marketplace card is a finished layout — the volume, the piece count and "gift wrapped" are printed on the image — and a square crop cut off exactly those lines
- A product description now runs the full width under both columns. Held inside the buy-box column it stretched that column alone, so a product with one photo showed half a page of nothing where the gallery ended
- The scrollbar under the main photo is gone; the gallery is paged by its thumbnails and by a finger

1.15.0 / August 17, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The shop's own feed now states how many of each product are left, and import reads it. Copying a catalogue from one instance of the shop into another used to spread a single made-up number over everything, because the feed standard carries availability as a flag and no quantity at all: a product with one left and a product with twenty arrived identical, which is an oversell waiting for a buyer. A feed from anywhere else states no quantity and behaves exactly as before
- An import no longer keeps its coefficient as the shop's own. A shop that takes catalogues from several suppliers converts one of them out of another currency, and that number, remembered, repriced every other supplier's goods on their next import — by a factor of twenty-eight in the case that found it

1.14.2 / August 17, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The Wildberries sandbox no longer waits out a DNS timeout on every check: there is no common-api-sandbox host at all, so the test contour skips the seller-info call instead of falling back to production with a token that does not belong there. Found the first time a real test token was plugged in

1.14.1 / August 16, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The admin showed roubles and the browser's language until the page was reloaded: the shop's own currency and the owner's language were fetched on a session that already existed, and reaching the admin through the login form skipped that. A Belarusian shop priced its goods in the wrong money for the whole first visit

1.14.0 / August 16, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Import reads a supplier's own Excel price list, not only our CSV template. Columns are found by their headers and the header row by its content, so the logo, the phone number and three empty rows a price list opens with are no obstacle. Checked on a live 143 MB file: 23 699 products in five seconds
- Pictures pasted into the cells come out with their rows and are written straight to the shop's uploads. A photo inside a spreadsheet has no link to fall back on, so it is saved now or lost, and asking an owner to extract twenty thousand of them by hand is the same as saying no
- One file field, two formats: a spreadsheet is recognised by its own bytes, so there is no second radio button to think about
- The Wildberries tab, a channel of its own next to Ozon: connect the account, link products to cards by the seller's article, publish and unpublish by selection, set a platform price flat or by the markup ladder, sync stock and prices, and read the log of sales the platform made. Nothing is shared with Ozon except the ladder arithmetic — a channel is a vertical slice, and the differences below are why
- Linking reads both platform keys off the card: stock is set per barcode, price per nmID. Our own catalogue never carries a barcode, and it never has to — a price list from Excel or a YML feed links by article exactly as it does on Ozon
- A card with several sizes has several barcodes and one article, so which size a product means is not knowable. Those are reported as unlinked instead of guessed at, and only a catalogue imported from Wildberries itself (whose articles carry the size) links straight through
- Prices on Wildberries are accepted as an upload task and confirmed later, so "sent" and "accepted" are two different facts: the tab shows how many prices are in flight, a confirmed task becomes the new baseline, and a task the platform never answers releases its products after an hour instead of pinning them forever
- Sizes of one card that want different prices push nothing at all and say why. Wildberries takes one price per card, and picking one size's number would quietly reprice the others
- A sandbox switch in the settings: the tab talks to the Wildberries test hosts with a test token, so the connection can be proven without touching real goods, orders or balance
- The platform's own message is truncated and the token is stripped out of it before it reaches a log or the database: an API that echoes a request back would otherwise carry the key into our files
- Consecutive calls are spaced out. Wildberries meters per method, and a burst costs a 429 that lasts far longer than the pause
- Errors now carry the time of the next attempt. A failure with no "and then what" reads as if the sync had given up

1.13.2 / August 16, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A photo still hosted on the supplier's server is marked with a warning badge, in the product list and in the product dialog alike. Imported photos are links, and a link dies with whoever holds it; until now nothing on the screen told the owner which pictures they do not actually own a copy of
- A product with no photo shows the storefront's own "no photo" stub in the admin list instead of a blank gap, so the column stays a column and the owner sees what a shopper would see
- The badge explains itself: as soon as one is on screen, a line above the table says what it means and that "Fill in" is what brings the photos in. A mark nobody can decode is a decoration

1.13.1 / August 15, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The import screen says out loud whose photos and descriptions a feed carries, and asks the owner to confirm they may use them before the button works. It arrives with an Excel price list and a marketplace account just as it does with a feed, and it is also what makes the import an instruction from the owner rather than something we did to their shop

1.13.0 / August 15, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The Categories tab: the storefront's sections are now yours to keep. Create a section (including an empty one, before anything is in it), rename it, move it under another parent, set the order of siblings, hide a branch from the storefront, delete it. The tree is on the left, the form on the right — the layout every catalogue admin uses
- Renaming or moving a section carries its products and its whole branch with it, in one transaction, and the old address answers 301 instead of 404: tidying the tree must not throw away what a page earned in search
- Deleting a section is not deleting what stands on it: the products and the subcategories move up to the parent, and a root section's goods simply lose their category
- A hidden section and everything below it disappears from the catalogue, the category index and the sitemap
- Two names that would produce one storefront address are refused with a message instead of one page quietly replacing the other
- A buyer leaves a phone or an email, whichever they prefer: the checkout asks for both and refuses only when neither is filled — an order nobody can be reached about is a lost sale that looks like a sale. An email also gets the buyer a confirmation letter, so a shop that took an order no longer answers with silence
- The cart row got the controls every shop has: minus, plus and a cross. Until now it had an "Update" button and deleting a line meant typing a zero into the quantity — a rule nobody guesses, in the one place where a shop loses a sale it already had
- Orders can be deleted, by an explicit list of rows and with a confirmation that says what disappears. Stock is not returned by a delete — an order that still holds goods should be cancelled first. The email is in the admin list and in the CSV journal
- A text of your own on a category page: it shows above the goods and becomes the page description for search engines, instead of the same generated sentence on every page
- The category field in the product dialog took a quarter of a line and cut the path off; it now has a row of its own

1.12.0 / August 15, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- A page for every category: /c/tekstil/spalnya/kpb-evro with its own title, description, breadcrumbs and listing. A search for "купить КПБ евро оптом" lands on a page about that, not on the catalogue with a query parameter; the old ?category= address redirects to the new one permanently
- Categories now arrive with the catalogue. Every source fills them: the tree of a YML feed, the Ozon taxonomy (one request per import, not one per card), the WB subject directory, and a price-list cell written as "Текстиль > Спальня". Until now the column existed and no importer ever wrote to it — a shop of 20,000 products had one category page, empty
- A category is a path, so a parent shows everything below it: "Текстиль" lists the goods of "Текстиль/Спальня/КПБ", which gives the shop landing pages for broad searches as well as narrow ones
- Category pages and the category index are in the sitemap, each with its own lastmod
- Filters for the buyer, still without a line of JavaScript: sort by price or name, "in stock only". The state lives in the address, so a filtered list can be sent to someone; every variant points a canonical at the plain page, so search engines see one page and not five
- HEAD returned 405 for every storefront page: a GET-only route on the outer router answered before the storefront could. Monitoring, link checkers and some crawlers saw a shop that looked broken

1.11.0 / August 14, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- Legal details for the shop: a free-form block in the Profile that goes into the footer of every storefront page and into schema.org/Organization markup, so search engines and buyers can see whom they are dealing with
- A "Delivery and payment" page: delivery, payment and returns written as plain text in the Profile, published at /info, linked from the footer and listed in the sitemap. Neither Yandex nor Google accepts a shop into shopping results without published terms; an empty field means no page and no link
- Product feeds for Yandex (/yml.xml) and Google Merchant Center (/gmc.xml)
- Search engines and analytics: Google Analytics and Yandex Metrika ids in the Profile, and the storefront still carries no script at all while both are empty
- Order emails come from the shop, not from a mailbox: the sender name is the shop name, and the address can differ from the SMTP login for owners whose provider forbids sending as another user
- A product with no photos shows a placeholder instead of an empty column half a page tall
- Instance stats in the admin: CPU, memory, disks, network, uptime, the size of the database and of the uploads directory
- New shops are handed a one-time invite link instead of a printed password
- An uploaded logo larger than needed is shrunk on upload
- The storefront's html lang follows the shop language
- Search-engine verification fields are gone from the Profile: proving domain ownership is the owner's business, and every registrar and search engine offers a way that needs no field in the shop
- The admin keeps one page width across tabs, and a product dialog scrolls itself instead of the page behind it

1.10.0 / August 10, 2026
[Alexandr Topilski]
- The catalogue grid shows small copies of the photos instead of the supplier's originals: a page of sixty products used to pull megabytes to draw thumbnails. Measured on a real catalogue, a 197 KB photo becomes 16 KB. The product page still shows the original
- Photos already in a shop get their small copies from the second stage of "Fill in", with the same progress and the same stop button
- Uploaded files are served with a long, immutable cache: a repeat visitor downloads no photos at all
- The admin panel had the storefront's icon in its browser tab — the seller's initial, generated for their shop — because it asked for /favicon.svg by absolute path

